WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.500
A great performance can leave a permanent
impression on moviegoers.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:08.650
Then again, bad acting can leave a similarly
permanent impression...but for very different

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:09.650
reasons.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:12.980
Over the years, we've seen plenty of awful
acting, and these scenes represent some of

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:14.059
the very worst.

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:16.420
"No, not the bees.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:17.420
Not the bees!

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:18.420
Ahhhhh!

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.130
They're in my eyes!

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:21.340
My eyes!

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.080
Ahhh!

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.400
Ahhhhh!"

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.760
Here are some of our favorite poorly acted
movie scenes...

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.349
Power of Law

00:00:30.349 --> 00:00:34.730
Of all the ridiculous scenes in the Sylvester
Stallone-starring dystopian disaster Judge

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:40.300
Dredd, none play out quite as badly as the
long-awaited confrontation between Judge Dredd

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.829
and his brother Rico at the end of the movie.

00:00:42.829 --> 00:00:47.580
What begins as a slow-burning war of words
quickly escalates to the point where Stallone

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.879
and Armand Assante forget how to pronounce
basic English.

00:00:50.879 --> 00:00:52.329
"Why did you judge me?

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:53.489
"You killed innocent people."

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:54.489
"The means to an end."

00:00:54.489 --> 00:00:55.489
"You started a massacre!"

00:00:55.489 --> 00:00:56.569
"I caused a revolution."

00:00:56.569 --> 00:00:57.689
"You betrayed the Lawrgh!"

00:00:57.689 --> 00:00:58.680
"Law!!"

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:02.440
Not like Stallone has that great a grasp of
English to begin with…

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:08.580
(speaking gibberish)

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:10.720
What kind of places?

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.910
Pretty much any scene from Showgirls is bad
enough to make this list.

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:18.160
But the one that always sticks out as being
the Very Worst happens to be toward the beginning

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:22.200
of the movie, when Nomi meets her future roommate,
Molly, for the first time.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:27.790
As we saw on Saved by the Bell, Elizabeth
Berkley tends to crank each line up to 11.

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:30.970
"I'm so excited.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.430
I'm so...scared."

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:34.480
(cries)

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:38.560
Berkley might have been channeling that very
scary moment when performing in this scene,

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.500
when she vigorously files her nails, violently
stabs her beverage and pours ketchup over

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.390
french fries as if she's auditioning for a
commercial about anger management.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:52.370
Eventually, Molly asks where Nomi's from...and
that's when things get really dramatic…

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.060
"Where are you from?"

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:57.620
"Back east."

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:58.760
"From where back east?"

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.280
(takes anger out on fast food)

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:02.560
"Different places!"

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.500
That's a pretty extreme reaction, huh?

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.360
But maybe it's not the line of questioning
that's got her upset — maybe she just doesn't

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.060
have the right snacks.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.350
"Doggy Chow…

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:16.190
I used to love Doggy chow."

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:20.180
"I used to love Doggy Chow too."

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.060
Feeling burned

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:25.991
Nicolas Cage's performance in The Wicker Man
is so bad, people who haven't even seen the

00:02:25.991 --> 00:02:29.360
movie are able to quote infamous lines, like
this one.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.670
"Now why in the hell would you let them to
do a sick thing like that?"

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:37.420
Things get particularly loopy when Cage's
character — a policeman who interrogates

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:42.120
a group of neo-Pagans after his daughter goes
missing — discovers a burned doll he believes

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.780
belonged to his daughter.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:47.680
That's when we get a glimpse into the "Nicolas
Cage School of Interrogation."

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:48.910
"How'd it get burned?

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:50.130
How'd it get burned?

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:51.360
How'd it get burned?

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.360
"I don't know!"

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.400
"Tell me!"

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:56.020
Anakin’s beach blues

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:02.260
Even after we got The Force Awakens, the ill-advised
Star Wars prequels continue to linger painfully

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:05.700
in fans' souls, like some kind of cosmic penance.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.290
(nonsensical Jar Jar jargon)

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:12.930
And few scenes are as hatefully remembered
as Hayden Christensen's chat with Padme in

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:16.550
Attack of the Clones about — that's right
— sand.

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:20.550
Because when you've got a movie with lightsabers
and an army of clones marching around with

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:25.540
laser guns, and galactic stability hangs in
the balance, we need to stop and talk about

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:28.090
how annoying it is when sand gets in your
crack.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:30.180
"I don’t like sand.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:36.120
It’s coarse and rough and irritating and
it gets everywhere."

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.720
Over a decade later, people are still joking
about it.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.060
Talk about a scene that has legs!

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:49.630
Ooh...just realizing about that whole "has
legs" thing.

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:50.630
Really insensitive.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:52.460
Sorry about that.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.280
What's happening?

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:56.950
What do you do when you try to calm down a
crazy lady?

00:03:56.950 --> 00:04:00.920
You deny her accusations with the convictions
and believability of a four-year-old boy.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.920
At least, that was Mark Wahlberg's plan during
his confrontation with Betty Buckley in M.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.540
Night Shyamalan's The Happening.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:08.960
"Planning on stealing something?"

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.200
"No, ma'am, we're not."

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.460
"Plan on murdering me in my sleep?"

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:15.460
"What?

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:16.460
No!"

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:20.250
Even Wahlberg himself admitted the movie sucked
during a press conference for The Fighter

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:21.250
two years later:

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:23.940
"You can't blame me for wanting to try to
play a science teacher, you know?

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.130
I wasn't playing a cop or a crook."

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:30.050
Mark Wahlberg, doing a bad job of acting in
a forgettable movie?

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:31.080
"What?

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:32.110
No!"

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:33.690
Don't feed the trolls

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.020
Picture this scenario.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:38.320
You realize you're about to be eaten alive
by trolls.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:39.320
What do you do?

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:40.320
What do you say?

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:44.690
Maybe you take a cue from Arnold, the nerdy
teenager from Troll 2, and do something like

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:45.290
this?

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:49.580
"They're eating her... and then they're going
to eat me...

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:56.040
Oh my goddd!!"

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:58.520
Alas, it was too late to save Arnold.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:04:59.530
"What?

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:00.530
No!"

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:01.530
Sorry, Mark Wahlberg.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.510
There was nothing we could do.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:06.790
And of course...The Room

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:11.320
Skip to any random scene in The Room, Tommy
Wiseau's writing, acting, and directing debut

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:15.590
from 2003, and it's painfully bad… in the
very best way.

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:16.950
"I did not hit her!

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:18.090
I did not!

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:19.230
Oh, hi Mark!"

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:24.240
The film is so poorly constructed that it's
been dubbed "The Citizen Kane of bad movies,"

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.550
making it hard to narrow it down to just one
laughable scene.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.790
"Chicken, Peter, you're just a little chicken!"

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:38.900
But the most popular, best of the worst moment
came when Wiseau's cuckolded Johnny confronts

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:43.260
his thought-to-be-cheating fiance, Lisa, in
this now iconic exchange:

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.990
"You're lying, I merely hit you.

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.139
You're tearing me apart, Lisa!"

00:05:48.139 --> 00:05:54.330
Believe it or not, according to Wiseau's costar
and friend Greg Sestero, the moment was inspired

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:59.510
by James Dean, who uttered a virtually identical
line in Rebel Without a Cause.

00:05:59.510 --> 00:06:02.010
"You're tearing me apart!"

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:05.140
"You're tearing me apart, Lisa!"

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:06.740
Who performed it better?

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:08.080
We think it's time for Tommy to celebrate.

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:10.670
"Do you want me to order a pizza?"

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:12.810
"Whatever, I don't care."

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:13.800
"I already ordered a pizza."

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.460
"You think about everything."

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.460
Thanks for watching!

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:20.650
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:24.620
And leave us a comment to let us know which
scenes you think we should've had on this

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:25.120
list.

